## Slots

### {SLOT_1}
Kind: value

This slot is the right-hand side of the assignment in the UPDATE of Minor_in.DNO. It must be the procedure parameter para_DNO exactly, with no transformation. The value is the integer argument supplied by the caller. Do not quote it, do not add arithmetic, and do not use a column reference.

### {SLOT_2}
Kind: condition

This slot is the condition controlling the Department UPDATE inside the loop over the Department cursor. It must compare the DPhone column of the current Department row fetched into the record variable rec2 with the procedure parameter para_DPhone. Use the PostgreSQL exact equality operator =, with the field from rec2 that preserves the exact column name DPhone as the left operand and para_DPhone as the right operand. The comparison must use PostgreSQL equality semantics: values must be exactly equal, and null is not equal to any value, including another null. Do not use case-insensitive or pattern matching.

### {SLOT_3}
Kind: value

This slot is the value assigned to Department.Division in the UPDATE. It must be the procedure parameter para_Division exactly, with no transformation or added characters. The value is the text argument supplied by the caller.

### {SLOT_4}
Kind: value

This slot is the value assigned to Member_of.DNO in the later UPDATE. It must be the procedure parameter para_DNO exactly, with no transformation. The value is the integer argument supplied by the caller. Do not quote it, do not add arithmetic, and do not use a column reference.

### {SLOT_5}
Kind: predicate

This slot is the predicate restricting which Member_of rows are updated by the UPDATE in step 5. It must compare the Appt_Type column of Member_of with the procedure parameter para_Appt_Type using the PostgreSQL exact equality operator =. Use the column with the exact name Appt_Type as the left operand and para_Appt_Type as the right operand. The comparison must use PostgreSQL equality semantics: values must be exactly equal, and null is not equal to any value, including null. Do not use case-insensitive or pattern matching.
